<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlemcen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tlemcen is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> that was an important commercial and political centre of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The Zayyanid seizure of Tlemcen around 1236 carved the central Maghreb out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> domain. It then became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanid dynasty</a>, one of the successor states of the Almohads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlemcen.jpg" alt="Tlemcen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tonowari · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_Mosqu%C3%A9e_de_Tlemcen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zayyanids established themselves at <b>Tlemcen</b> around 1236, carving out the central Maghreb, and the fall of Córdoba (1236) and Seville (1248) to Ferdinand III of Castile ended centuries of Muslim rule in the cities most closely associated with Andalusian civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Constantine and Bejaia in 1230 and Algiers in 1235, and in 1242 forced the Zayyanid ruler of <b>Tlemcen</b>, Yaghmurasan, to acknowledge his overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Idris I spread his authority across much of northern Morocco, as far as <b>Tlemcen</b>, but he was wholly dependent on the Awraba leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>With this army the Marinids took Marrakesh in 1269, besieged <b>Tlemcen</b> for nine years from 1299 and fought in Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Oruç took Algiers, had its ruler Salim al-Tumi killed according to most accounts, and extended his control inland as far as <b>Tlemcen</b>, where he was killed by Spanish forces in 1518.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the great Berber revolt against the Umayyad Caliphate from 740, they embraced Sufri Kharijism, and around 742 their leader Abu Qurra established a state at <b>Tlemcen</b>, where he was proclaimed imam.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ifran-emirate">Banu Ifran emirate</a></li></ul></section>
